[[Image:Convex quadrilateral.svg|thumb|Trapezoid (2) (UK)]]

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|grc|τραπέζιον||irregular quadrilateral|lit=a little table}} + {{m|en|-oid||resembling}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/trəˈpiːzɔɪd/|a=RP}}
* {{enPR|tră'pəzoid}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈtɹæpəzɔɪd/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-trapezoid.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geometry|US|Canada}} A ([[convex]]) [[quadrilateral]] with two (non-adjacent) parallel sides.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2023|author=[[w:Brandon Taylor (writer)|Brandon Taylor]]|title={{w|The Late Americans}}|publisher=Jonathan Cape|page=178|passage=There was a '''trapezoid''' of light on his shoulder, some bright fragment torn from a greater plane.}}
# {{lb|en|geometry|British|AU|NZ}} A [[convex]] [[quadrilateral]] with no sides parallel.
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The [[trapezoid bone]] of the [[wrist]].
# {{lb|en|ice hockey}} The marked trapezoidal area immediately behind the goal line and the goal itself within which the [[goaltender]] may touch the [[puck]].

